1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,360
How can an earthquake beneath
the ocean create a tsunami?

2
00:00:03,815 --> 00:00:07,905
At some plate boundaries,
one tectonic plate slides beneath another.

3
00:00:08,185 --> 00:00:10,215
Their edges can lock, storing strain.

4
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:15,260
When the fault suddenly slips,
the seafloor can rise or fall.

5
00:00:15,545 --> 00:00:19,475
This movement displaces the
entire column of water above it.

6
00:00:20,717 --> 00:00:23,547
Gravity pulls the disturbed
water back toward balance.

7
00:00:23,932 --> 00:00:28,562
Waves spread outward in all directions,
carrying energy across the ocean.

8
00:00:29,883 --> 00:00:33,093
In deep water,
tsunami waves have very long wavelengths.

9
00:00:33,598 --> 00:00:37,388
Their height may be small,
so passing ships might barely notice them.

10
00:00:38,683 --> 00:00:41,153
Near shore, shallower
water slows the waves.

11
00:00:41,538 --> 00:00:44,728
Their wavelengths shorten and
their height can grow dramatically.

12
00:00:45,163 --> 00:00:47,633
Water surges inland, flooding the coast.

13
00:00:49,083 --> 00:00:50,993
A tsunami is a series of waves,

14
00:00:51,383 --> 00:00:53,033
and the first may not be largest.

15
00:00:53,453 --> 00:00:57,703
The key trigger is sudden displacement
of a large volume of water.

